WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.449 --> 00:00:05.850
So if you read the headlines, it kind of seems
like the world is a terrible place, full of

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:10.570
violence, despair, and war. But it turns out
war is actually declining. We live in the

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:15.150
most peaceful time in human history. There's
lots of reasons why, but here are three of

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.860
the biggest ones. First, the spread of democracy
around the world. International relations

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.480
scholars have found consistently that democracies
don't fight wars with each other. Now why

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.120
is that true? There might be plenty of reasons
but one big one is that people who live in

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.320
democracies think it's wrong to start wars
with other democracies. They're legitimate

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:37.880
governments -- it's wrong to attack them.
But there's a flipside to this. Democracies

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:42.650
do often fight wars with autocracies. Luckily,
most of the world's countries are democracies now,

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:47.660
so the democratic peace is probably making
the world a much more peaceful place. Second

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:52.260
big reason why war is declining is nuclear
deterrence. Nuclear weapons, obviously big

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.079
and scary. However, nuclear deterrence may
have prevented a devastating war between the

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:01.340
United States and the Soviet Union. Everyone
recognized that they would lose. Statistical

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:06.009
evidence suggests that that is true -- that
most countries are too scared of the consequences

00:01:06.009 --> 00:01:10.509
of nuclear war to fight one. There's a dark
side, and not just the risk of global annihilation

00:01:10.509 --> 00:01:17.139
from an accident. If a country gets nuclear
weapons, they feel rather safer in being aggressive

00:01:17.139 --> 00:01:22.399
in little ways, you know, small conflicts
or bullying around their non-nuclear neighbors.

00:01:22.399 --> 00:01:27.119
This weird paradox, that nuclear weapons make
the world more violent and more peaceful at

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:32.389
the same time, is called the Stability-Instability
Paradox. The third reason that war has declined

00:01:32.389 --> 00:01:36.340
has been the spread of the idea of national
sovereignty. This idea is hundreds of years

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:40.959
old, the idea that you shouldn't interfere
inside the borders of another state. But people

00:01:40.959 --> 00:01:44.729
didn't take the idea too seriously, because
stealing new land used to be a major cause of

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:49.479
war. Think American European colonialism or
World War II. But after World War II, nations

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:54.840
pledged to stop. Since 1976 there hasn't been
a single successful war of conquest, except

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.789
for maybe Russia in Crimea, really recently.
Again though, sovereignty has a flip side.

00:01:59.789 --> 00:02:03.819
Sometimes governments go to war against their
own people, and poor, weak governments often

00:02:03.819 --> 00:02:07.409
collapse into civil war. Sovereignty makes
it hard for the international community to

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:11.120
intervene to stop either of those kinds of
wars. But on the whole, when you see those

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.269
terrible headlines, remember the world's way
better off than it ever has been. There's

00:02:15.269 --> 00:02:19.930
less war and less violence than almost any
other time in human history. 

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:23.830
That's something worth celebrating.

